\\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 8 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{red}1 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{red}7 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{red}6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{red}4 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}}\\end{array} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  &  & \\end{array}}\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the $\\color{blue}2$ factors.\n\nThere is ${\\color{purple}{1}}$ decimal place in $4.2$. Therefore, the exponent $(4.2)^{\\color{blue}2}$ will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{1}}\\times {\\color{blue}{2}} = \\color{red}2\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $1764$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{red}2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n17\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{64}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}2}} \\text{ digits}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(4.2)^2 = 17.64\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147185 :question/math-academy-id "e-14482" :question/problem "Calculate the value of $(3.01)^2$." :question/worked-solution "First, by writing the expression with multiplication signs, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3.01)^{2} &= 3.01 \\cdot 3.01_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{blue}{2}}[/math] times)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rlrl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\begin{array}{ccccccc}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{lightgray}\\begin{array}{c}\\displaystyle \\boxed{ {\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\boxed{ {\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\boxed{ {\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}}}\\end{array} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{lightgray}\\begin{array}{c}\\displaystyle \\boxed{ {\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\boxed{ {\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\boxed{ {\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}}}\\end{array} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} & \\displaystyle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 3 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 1 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\times  \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 3 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 1 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 3 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 1 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} + \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} + \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 9 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 3 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{red}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{red}9 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{red}0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{red}6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{red}0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{red}1 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}}\\end{array} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  &  & \\end{array}}\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the $\\color{blue}2$ factors.\n\nThere are ${\\color{purple}{2}}$ decimal places in $3.01$.\nTherefore, the exponent $(3.01)^{\\color{blue}2}$ will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{2}}\\times {\\color{blue}{2}} = \\color{red}4\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $90601$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{red}4$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n9\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{0601}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}4}} \\text{ digits}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(30.1)^2 = 9.0601\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171752 :question/math-academy-id "e-14966" :question/problem "Prove by contradiction that $45x+27y \\neq 1$ for any $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$." :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP: \\quad \\forall x, y\\in\\mathbb Z,\\, 45x+27y \\neq 1\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P: \\quad \\exists x, y\\in\\mathbb Z,\\,45x+27y = 1\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that there exists $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that $45x+27y = 1$.\n\nThe idea is to factor the expression on the left-hand side and consider the product as factors of the integer on the right-hand side.\n\n> Factoring the expression on the left-hand side gives\n> $45x + 27y|= 1; 9(5x + 3y)_(⏟)_(a)|= 1$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\na=5x+3y\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have $9a=1$, which, by the definition of divisibility, means $9 \\:\\mid\\: 1$.\n\n> Let $a =5x+3y$. Since $x$ and $y$ are integers, we have that $a$ is an integer.\n> Hence, we have\n> $9(5x + 3y)_(⏟)_(a)|= 1; 9a|= 1$,\n> which means $9 \\:\\mid\\: 1$. But this is a contradiction since $9 \\:\\nmid\\: 1$.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our assumption that $\\lnot P$ is true is incorrect. Hence, $P$ is true, and we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $45x+27y \\neq 1$ for any $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203724 :question/math-academy-id "e-14968" :question/problem "Prove by contradiction that $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is irrational. You may assume without proof that $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational." :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP{:} \\quad \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is rational.\n\nSince we're assuming\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\nis rational, we can express it as the ratio of two non-zero integers:\n\n> Then $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the equation to give a radical on one side and a fraction on the other.\n\n> So, we have\n> $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}|= \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{3}|= (2a^(⏞)^(c))/(b)$.\n\nNow, if we define the integer $c=2a$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}=\\dfrac{c}{b}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.\n\n> Let $c=2a$. Since $2$ and $a$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{c}{b}$,\n> which is rational.\n> But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is irrational."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147187 :question/math-academy-id "e-15037" :question/problem "Calculate the value of $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4$." :question/worked-solution "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4$ with multiplication signs:\n$\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^{\\color{blue}{4}} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{3}{8}}_{\\large { { {\\color{blue}4}} \\text{ times}}}$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$(\\frac{3}{8})^{4}|= \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8}; = \\frac{3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3}{8 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 8}; = (3 \\cdot 3^(⏞)^(9) \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3^(⏞)^(9))/(8 \\cdot 8_(⏟)_(64) \\cdot 8 \\cdot 8_(⏟)_(64)); = \\frac{9 \\cdot 9}{64 \\cdot 64}; = \\frac{81}{4096}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{81}{4\\,096}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171778 :question/math-academy-id "e-15757" :question/problem "Consider the independent random variables $Y_1, Y_2, \\ldots, Y_{10}$ where $Y_i\\sim \\mathrm{B}(5,0.3)$ for $1\\leq i \\leq 10$. What is the distribution of $Y=Y_1+Y_2+\\cdots + Y_{10}$?\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n| Distribution | $M(t)$ | $t$-domain |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $U \\{x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{n} \\left(e^{x_1t} + e^{x_2t} + \\ldots + e^{x_nt} \\right)$ | $t\\in\\mathbb R$ |\n| $\\textrm{Bernoulli}(p)$ | $1-p+pe^t$ | $t\\in\\mathbb R$ |\n| $B(n,p)$ | $(1-p+pe^t)^n$ | $t\\in\\mathbb R$ |\n| $\\textrm{Geom}(p)$ | $\\dfrac{pe^t}{1-(1-p)e^t}$ | $t<-\\ln (1-p)$ |\n| $\\textrm{NB}(n,p)$ | $\\left(\\dfrac{pe^t}{1-(1-p)e^t}\\right)^n$ | $t<-\\ln (1-p)$ |\n| $\\text{Po}(\\lambda)$ | $e^{\\lambda(e^t-1)}$ | $t\\in\\mathbb R$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a random variable's probability distribution is uniquely determined by its moment-generating function (MGF). Therefore, if the MGF of a random variable $X$ matches that of a known distribution, then $X$ has the same distribution.\n\nFor a binomial random variable $Y_i \\sim \\textrm{B}(n,p)$, we have\n\n$$\nM_{Y_i}(t) = (1-p+pe^t)^n\n$$\n\nIn our case, since $Y_i \\sim \\textrm{B}(5,0.3)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{Y_{i}}(t) &= (1 - 0.3 + 0.3e^{t})^{5} \\\\\n&= (0.7 + 0.3e^{t})^{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf\n\n$$\nY = Y_1+Y_2+\\cdots + Y_{10}\n$$\n\nwhere $Y_1, Y_2,\\ldots Y_{10}$ are independent random variables with moment-generating function $M_{Y_i}(t)$, then the moment-generating function of $Y$ is\n\n$$\nM_Y(t) = M_{Y_1}(t) \\cdot M_{Y_2}(t) \\cdots M_{Y_{10}}(t)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{Y}(t) &= (0.7 + 0.3e^{t})^{5} \\cdot (0.7 + 0.3e^{t})^{5}⋯(0.7 + 0.3e^{t})^{5}^(⏞)^(10times) \\\\\n&= (0.7 + 0.3e^{t})^{50}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $M_Y(t)$ is the MGF of a binomial random variable with $n = 50$ and $p= 0.3$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $Y\\sim B(50,0.3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203878 :question/math-academy-id "e-18853" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n(r - 9)z + 2 = -1\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-9)z+2 & =-1 \\\\ (r-9)z+2-2 & =-1-2 \\\\ \\underbrace{(r-9)}_{a}z & =\\underbrace{-3}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nr - 9 = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r-9 & =0 \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212760 :question/math-academy-id "e-18868" :question/problem "Consider the expression\n$(y - 1)^3(y + 2) + (y + 4)(y - 1)^2$.\n\nThis expression can be written in the form\n$(y - 1)^2\\cdot g(y)$.\nFind the polynomial $g(y)$." :question/worked-solution "We're asked to factor the expression\n$(y - 1)^3(y + 2) + (y + 4)(y - 1)^2$.\n\nNotice that both terms in this expression contain a common factor of $(y - 1)^2$. So, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(y - 1)^2\\left[(y - 1)(y + 2) + (y + 4)\\right]\n$$\n\nNow, we simplify the expression inside the square brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\phantom{=} ( y - 1 {)}^{2} [ ( y - 1 ) ( y + 2 ) + ( y + 4 ) ] \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( y - 1 {)}^{2} [ {y}^{2} + y - 2 + y + 4 ] \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( y - 1 {)}^{2} \\cdot  \\underset{g ( y )}{\\underbrace{( {y}^{2} + 2 y + 2 )}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ng(y) = y^2 + 2y + 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212765 :question/math-academy-id "e-18876" :question/problem "Simplify the following expression:\n$\\dfrac{5}{2x^2-11x+5}+\\dfrac{7x}{x-5}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the denominator in the first term, $2x^2-11x+5$.\n\nTo factor this quadratic expression, we need to find two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 5=10\n$$\n\nand add to $-11$. These are $-10$ and $-1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 11x + 5 &= 2x^{2}- 10x - x_(⏟)_(-11x) + 5 \\\\\n&= (2x^{2} - 10x) + (-x + 5) \\\\\n&= 2x(x - 5) - 1(x - 5) \\\\\n&= (x - 5)(2x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2x^2-11x+5}+\\dfrac{7x}{x-5} = \\dfrac{5}{(x-5)(2x-1)}+\\dfrac{7x}{x-5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can put each rational expression over a common denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of the second fraction by $(2x-1)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + \\frac{7x}{x - 5} &= (5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + \\frac{2x - 1}{2x - 1} \\cdot \\frac{7x}{x - 5} \\\\\n&= (5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + (7x(2x - 1))/((x - 5)(2x - 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the rational expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + (7x(2x - 1))/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) &= (5 + 7x(2x - 1))/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) \\\\\n&= (5 + 14x^{2} - 7x)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) \\\\\n&= (14x^{2} - 7x + 5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212835 :question/math-academy-id "e-19008" :question/problem "Consider the expression\n$(2a-7)^3(a+2) + (3a+4)(7-2a)^2$.\n\nThis expression can be written in the form\n$(2a-7)^2\\cdot g(a)$.\nFind the polynomial $g(a)$." :question/worked-solution "We're asked to factor the expression\n$(2a-7)^3(a+2) + (3a+4)(7-2a)^2$.\n\nNotice that\n\n- the first term in this expression contains a factor of $(2a-7)^2$, and\n- the second term contains a factor of $(7-2a)^2$.\n\nThese factors differ only by the sign:\n\n$$\n7-2a = -(2a-7)\n$$\n\nHowever, the squaring eliminates the minus sign:\n\n$$\n(7-2a)^2 = (2a-7)^2\n$$\n\nSo, we can simply rewrite the second term:\n$(2a-7)^3(a+2) + (3a+4)(2a-7)^2$\n\nNow, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(2a-7)^2 \\left[(2a-7)(a+2) + (3a+4)\\right]\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the expression inside the square brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2a - 7)^{2}[(2a - 7)(a + 2) + (3a + 4)] &=  \\\\\n(2a - 7)^{2}[2a^{2} + 4a - 7a - 14 + 3a + 4] &=  \\\\\n&= (2a - 7)^{2}(2a^{2} - 10)_(⏟)_(g(a))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ng(a) = 2a^2 - 10\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186224846 :question/math-academy-id "e-19365" :question/problem "The solutions to $m^2 + 9m + 14 = 0$ are $r$ and $s$, with $r < s$, while the solutions to $m^2 + 5m + 4 = 0$ are $t$ and $u$ with $t < u$. If the solutions to $m^2 + 14m + k = 0$, where $k$ is a constant, are $r + t$ and $s + u$, what is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "Let's solve the first two equations:\n\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 9m + 14 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $14$ and add to $9$. These two numbers are $7$ and $2$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$m^{2} + 9m + 14|= 0; m^{2}+ 7m + 2m_(⏟)_(9m) + 14|= 0; m(m + 7) + 2(m + 7)|= 0; (m + 7)(m + 2)|= 0$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}m + 7 = 0 & ⇒m =-7 \\\\ m + 2 = 0 & ⇒m =-2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the solutions $r$ and $s$, with $r < s$, are the following:\n$r =-7ands =-2$\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 5m + 4 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $4$ and add to $5$. These two numbers are $1$ and $4$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$m^{2} + 5m + 4|= 0; m^{2}+ m + 4m_(⏟)_(5m) + 4|= 0; m(m + 1) + 4(m + 1)|= 0; (m + 1)(m + 4)|= 0$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}m + 1 = 0 & ⇒m =-1 \\\\ m + 4 = 0 & ⇒m =-4\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the solutions $t$ and $u$, with $t < u$, are the following:\n$t =-4andu =-1$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr + t &= -7 + (-4) =-11, \\\\\ns + u &= -2 + (-1) =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are given that both $r+t$ and $s+u$ are solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nm^2 + 14m + k = 0\n$$\n\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\nm = s+u = -3\n$$\n\ninto this equation (it also works to substitute\n\n$$\nm=r+t=-11\n$$\n\n) and solving for $k$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-3)^{2} + 14(-3) + k &= 0 \\\\\n9 - 42 + k &= 0 \\\\\n- 33 + k &= 0 \\\\\nk &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216892 :question/math-academy-id "e-19726" :question/problem "A line intersects two parallel lines, forming four acute angles and four obtuse angles. The measure of one of the acute angles is $(96-5t)^\\circ$, and the sum of the measures of one of the obtuse angles and two of the acute angles is $(k-5t)^\\circ$. What is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "When a transversal intersects two parallel lines, it creates eight angles in total: four acute angles and four obtuse angles. All acute angles are congruent, and all obtuse angles are congruent, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b463497712cb58cf8ad57e55a177ca0d9c645207348232d5875f6e6976c49c3.png)\n\nLet $\\angle A$ be an acute angle and $\\angle O$ be an obtuse angle.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = (96 - 5t)^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe are told that the sum of one obtuse angle and two acute angles is $(k-5t)^\\circ$. Writing as an equation, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle O + 2m\\angle A = (k-5t)^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\angle A$ and $\\angle O$ are supplementary. Therefore, the left-hand side of the above equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠O + 2m∠A &= m∠O + m∠A_(⏟)_(180^{∘}) + m∠A \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} + (96 - 5t)^{∘} \\\\\n&= 276^{∘} - 5t^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can equate this to $(k-5t)^\\circ$ and solve for $k$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n276^{∘} - 5t^{∘} &= k^{∘} - 5t^{∘} \\\\\n276 &= k \\\\\nk &= 276\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186224898 :question/math-academy-id "e-20060" :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = {x}^{2} - 5 x + 3 \\\\ x - y = 2\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIf $(x_{1}, y_{1})$ and $(x_{2}, y_{2})$ are the two solutions to the system of equations above, what is the value of $y_{1} + y_{2}$?" :question/worked-solution "We are given a system of equations and asked to find $y_1 + y_2$, the sum of the $y$-coordinates of the two points where the graphs intersect.\n\nFirst, we substitute the expression for $y$ into the second equation and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= 2 \\\\\nx - (x^{2} - 5x + 3) &= 2 \\\\\n- x^{2} + 6x - 3 &= 2 \\\\\n- x^{2} + 6x - 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 6x + 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation by factoring. We begin by finding two numbers that multiply to $5$ and add to $-6$. These are $-1$ and $-5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 6x + 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 5x - x_(⏟)_(-6x) + 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 5) - 1(x - 5) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 5)(x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = 1\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y$ using\n\n$$\ny = x^2 - 5x + 3{:}\n$$\n\n- If $x_1 = 5$, then\n$y_1 = 5^2 - 5(5) + 3 = 25 - 25 + 3 = 3$\n- If $x_2 = 1$, then\n$y_2 = 1^2 - 5(1)+3=1 - 5 + 3 = -1$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\ny_1 + y_2 = 3 + (-1) = 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208402 :question/math-academy-id "e-20071" :question/problem "${y = 49; y = x^{2} - 2x + 1$\n\nThe graphs of the given equations in the $xy$-plane intersect at the point $(x, y)$. What is the possible value of $x$?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the following system:\n\n$$\n{y = 49; y = x^{2} - 2x + 1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y = 49$ into the second equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 &= 49 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 - 49 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x - 48 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation by factoring. We begin by finding two numbers that multiply to $-48$ and add to $-2$. These are $-8$ and $6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x - 48 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2}- 8x + 6x_(⏟)_(-2x) - 48 &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 8) + 6(x - 8) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 8)(x + 6) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x - 8 = 0 & orx + 6 = 0 \\\\ x = 8 & orx =-6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the possible value of $x$ is $8$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216980 :question/math-academy-id "e-20898" :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n\\left(x + y^2\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}x + \\left(xy + \\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=x+y^2,\\qquad Q(x,y)=xy+\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation." :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int {x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x[(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{({x}^{2}y+2)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{P}(x,y)=x^2+xy^2,\\qquad\n\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)=x^2y+2.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\widetilde{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}y+2)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+2y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\qquad G(x)=\\frac{x^3}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^3}{3}+\\frac{x^2y^2}{2}+2y=c.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221091 :question/math-academy-id "e-21048" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z$, given by\n$(r - 9)z + 2 = -1$\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r$?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n$ax = b$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n$$\nb\\neq 0\n$$\n\nIf $a = 0$, then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - 9)z + 2 &= -1 \\\\\n(r - 9)z + 2 - 2 &= -1 - 2 \\\\\n(r - 9)_(⏟)_(a)z &= -3_(⏟)_(b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n$r - 9 = 0$.\n\nSolving this for $r$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr - 9 &= 0 \\\\\nr &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186224993 :question/math-academy-id "e-21200" :question/problem "If $k$ is a solution of the equation $t^2 - t - 30 = 0$ and $k > 0$, what is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "To factor the left-hand side, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $-30$ and add to $-1$. These two numbers are $-6$ and $5$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} - t - 30 &= 0 \\\\\nt^{2}- 6t + 5t_(⏟)_(-t) - 30 &= 0 \\\\\nt(t - 6) + 5(t - 6) &= 0 \\\\\n(t - 6)(t + 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}t - 6 = 0 & ⇒t = 6 \\\\ t + 5 = 0 & ⇒t =-5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are given that $k$ is a solution to our equation, with $k \\gt 0$. Therefore, we conclude that $k = 6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186224994 :question/math-academy-id "e-21201" :question/problem "What is the positive solution to the equation $-p^2 - 3p + 4 = 0$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our equation in standard form and with a positive leading coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-p^{2} - 3p + 4 &= 0 \\\\\np^{2} + 3p - 4 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $-4$ and add to $3$. These two numbers are $4$ and $-1$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{2} + 3p - 4 &= 0 \\\\\np^{2}+ 4p - p_(⏟)_(3p) - 4 &= 0 \\\\\np(p + 4) - 1(p + 4) &= 0 \\\\\n(p + 4)(p - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}p + 4 = 0 & ⇒p =-4 \\\\ p - 1 = 0 & ⇒p = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the **positive** solution to the given equation is $p = 1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186224995 :question/math-academy-id "e-21202" :question/problem "What is the positive solution to the equation $4y^2 - 17y - 15 = 0$?\n\nExplanation:\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to $4 \\cdot (-15) = -60$ and add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$4y^{2} - 17y - 15|= 0; 4y^{2}- 20y + 3y_(⏟)_(-17y) - 15|= 0; 4y(y - 5) + 3(y - 5)|= 0; (y - 5)(4y + 3)|= 0$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y - 5 = 0 & ⇒y = 5 \\\\ 4y + 3 = 0 & ⇒y =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is $y = 5$." :question/worked-solution "To factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n4 \\cdot (-15) = -60\n$$\n\nand add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4y^{2} - 17y - 15 &= 0 \\\\\n4y^{2}- 20y + 3y_(⏟)_(-17y) - 15 &= 0 \\\\\n4y(y - 5) + 3(y - 5) &= 0 \\\\\n(y - 5)(4y + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y - 5 = 0 & ⇒y = 5 \\\\ 4y + 3 = 0 & ⇒y =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is $y = 5$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225062 :question/math-academy-id "e-21295" :question/problem "What is the positive solution to the equation $-12p^2 =-17p - 5$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our equation in standard form and with a positive leading coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12p^{2} &= -17p - 5 \\\\\n- 12p^{2} + 17p + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n12p^{2} - 17p - 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n12 \\cdot (-5) = -60\n$$\n\nand add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12p^{2} - 17p - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n12p^{2}- 20p + 3p_(⏟)_(-17p) - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n4p(3p - 5) + 1(3p - 5) &= 0 \\\\\n(4p + 1)(3p - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4p + 1 = 0 & ⇒p =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 3p - 5 = 0 & ⇒p = \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\np = \\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228831 :question/math-academy-id "e-21317" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = y^3 - x\\,y, \\qquad y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x = 1,$ the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = (y_\\text{new})^3 - 3y_\\text{new} - 1$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = 3y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + 1$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = 3y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 - 1$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new}$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $\\Delta x = 1$ is\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new} - y.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}-1 \\\\  & =3{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142712 :question/math-academy-id "e-2160" :question/problem "Denise sends a package to her father. The total volume of the package is $75$ cubic inches, and it is $6$ inches wide and $2.5$ inches high. By modeling the package as a rectangular prism, what is the length of the package according to this model?" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that the volume of a rectangular package can be calculated as\n\n$$\nV = \\text{length} \\times \\text{width} \\times \\text{height}\n$$\n\nand we are told that this volume is equal to $75 \\, \\text{in}^3$.\n\n![](images/32/32798500f85c9bc6dfc97b588d787ec9befd8a4f5b52aa0bc482588f71ca989f.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n$75 = \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\\times 6 \\times 2.5$\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n6 \\times 2.5 = 15\n$$\n\n, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n75 &= [math]\\,\\phantom{0}\\,[/math] \\cdot 6 \\cdot 2.5_(⏟)_(15) \\\\\n75 &= [math]\\,\\phantom{0}\\,[/math] \\cdot 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the relationship between multiplication and division, we obtain that the length of the package must be\n$\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}} = 75 \\div 15 = 5 \\, \\text{in}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147228 :question/math-academy-id "e-21779" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 5y' - 6y = p(x),\n$$\n\nwhose corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x)=e^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n![](images/58/58c8777459adc6e01e911d6b195e594bf2f6eca10d391c92245fea108452f911.svg)\n\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n![](images/69/695f7685566bb01af237cd41a612210a8b35605cfb8197838c3b0d5d851299ae.svg)\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between $u_1', u_2',$ and $p(x){:}$\n\n![](images/16/16d0290d86015f14f76025dce0b24ce19b8de65238de2e34a614cd4d2e85406e.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'.\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2 = e^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p =u_1e^{x}+u_2e^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p',$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' =u_1(e^{x})'+u_2 (e^{-6x})'.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-6y & =p(x) \\\\ ({y}_{p}^{′})^{′}+5{y}_{p}^{′}-6{y}_{p} & =p(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our expressions for $y_p$ and $y_p',$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-6x})^{′})}^{′}+5({u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-6x})^{′})-6({u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x}) & =p(x) \\\\ {({u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x})}^{′}+5{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-30{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x}-6{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x} & =p(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-6x}+36{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\,+5{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-30{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x}-6{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x} & =p(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group all terms proportional to $u_1$ and $u_2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}{{({e}^{x}+5{e}^{x}-6{e}^{x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{2}{{(36{e}^{-6x}-30{e}^{-6x}-6{e}^{-6x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-6x}=p(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the expressions in parentheses are both zero. Therefore, the equation reduces to\n\n$$\nu_1'e^{x} - 6u_2'e^{-6x} = p(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147259 :question/math-academy-id "e-2416" :question/problem "Which symbols could replace the empty box below to make the statement true?\n$4^2 \\,\\,\\square\\,\\, 1^7$\n\n1. $>$\n2. $<$\n3. $=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $4^2$. We have\n$4^{\\color{blue}2} = \\underbrace{4 \\times 4}_{\\large { { {\\color{blue}2}} \\text{ times}}} = 16.$\n\nThen, for $1^7$, we have\n\n$$\n1^{7} = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{red}7} [/math] times) = 1\n$$\n\nBecause $16 > 1$, the correct statement must be\n$4^2 > 1^7 \\,$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147260 :question/math-academy-id "e-2417" :question/problem "Which symbols could replace the empty box below to make the statement true?\n$\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 \\,\\,\\square\\,\\, \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^1$\n\n1. $>$\n2. $<$\n3. $=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1}{3})^{2} &= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{blue}2} [/math] times) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 \\cdot 1}{3 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, for\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^1\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{4})^{1} = \\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nBecause\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9} < \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nthe correct statement must be\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 < \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^1 \\,\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147261 :question/math-academy-id "e-2418" :question/problem "Which symbols could replace the empty box below to make the statement true?\n$-2^3 \\,\\,\\,\\square\\,-3^2$\n\n1. $>$\n2. $<$\n3. $=$" :question/worked-solution "**Watch Out!** Remember that,\n\n- $-2^3$ is shorthand for $-\\!\\left(2^3\\right)$,\n- similarly, $-3^2$ is shorthand for $-\\!\\left(3^2\\right)$.\n\nFirst, we calculate $-2^3$. We ignore the negative sign and calculate the positive exponent first:\n$2^{\\color{blue}3}  = \\underbrace{2 \\times 2 \\times 2}_{\\large { { {\\color{blue}3}} \\text{ times}}} = 8$\n\nTherefore, $-2^3 = -8$.\n\nNow, to compute $-3^2$, we again ignore the negative sign and calculate the positive exponent first:\n\n$$\n3^{2} = 3 \\cdot 3_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{red}2} [/math] times) = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-3^2 = -9$.\n\nFinally, let's look at the number line to compare $-8$ and $-9$.\n\n![](images/52/529d74b465ca32e111aac2a138222228b1aba1c06d7baf92bf8c5a84b3d78e09.png)\n\nSince $-8 > -9$, the correct statement must be\n$-2^3 > -3^2 \\,$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114975 :question/math-academy-id "e-2484" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf g(1)$ given that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $\\mathbf f(1) = \\left\\langle 1, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle$ and \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{t\\rightarrow 1} \\big[ 2\\mathbf f(t)-\\mathbf g(t) \\big] = \\left\\langle 2, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)-g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}2f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 1}[-g(t)] \\\\  & =2{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =2f(1)-g(1) \\\\  & =2{{⟨1,1,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{f(1)}-g(1) \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-2⟩-g(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{t\\rightarrow 1} \\big[ 2\\mathbf f(t)-\\mathbf g(t) \\big] = \\left\\langle 2, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $\\mathbf{g} (1)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,2,-2⟩-g(1) & =⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨2,2,-2⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110400 :question/math-academy-id "e-2711" :question/problem "Find the value of $x$ in the following figure.\n\n![](images/e7/e74d124c971c55844381cf9cedaa0e392e634e5ceda31d6af7a136426678f4db.png)" :question/worked-solution "*Method 1*\n\nLet's start by finding the value of the missing interior angle at $B.$ We use the fact that the interior and exterior angles are supplementary:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{73}^{∘}+m∠B & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={180}^{∘}-{73}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={107}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $x$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {41}^{∘}+{107}^{∘}+2x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {148}^{∘}+2x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={32}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={16}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n*Method 2*\n\nWe could rewrite the equation $m\\angle{A} +m\\angle{B} +m\\angle{C} = 180^\\circ$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠B}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]}}\\,\\,\\,=m∠A+m∠C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠B}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]}}\\,\\,\\, & =m∠A+m∠C \\\\ {73}^{∘} & ={41}^{∘}+2x \\\\ {32}^{∘} & =2x \\\\ {16}^{∘} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087484 :question/math-academy-id "e-3679" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac {3\\tan 35^\\circ}{2 - \\sec^2 35^\\circ}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the given expression using the identity\n\n$$\n\\sec^2 x = 1 + \\tan^2 x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3\\tan 35^{∘}}{2 - \\sec^{2} 35^{∘}} &= (3\\tan 35^{∘})/(2 - (1 + \\tan^{2} 35^{∘})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3\\tan 35^{∘}}{1 - \\tan^{2} 35^{∘}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {2\\tan x}{1 - \\tan^2 x} = \\tan 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=35^\\circ$ in the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2\\tan 35^{∘}}{1 - \\tan^{2} 35^{∘}} &= \\tan (2 \\cdot 35^{∘}) = \\tan 70^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3\\tan 35^{∘}}{1 - \\tan^{2} 35^{∘}} &= \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot [\\frac{2\\tan 35^{∘}}{1 - \\tan^{2} 35^{∘}}]_(⏟)_(\\tan 70^{∘}) = \\frac{3}{2}\\tan 70^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147333 :question/math-academy-id "e-3892" :question/problem "At a shopping mall, the rate at which people are entering the mall is given by\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per hour, and $t>0$ is the number of hours since the mall opened. In addition, people are leaving the mall at a steady rate of $25$ people per hour. \n\nI. Calculate the number of people that entered the mall in the first two hours.\n\nII. Calculate the net change in the number of people inside the mall in the first 2 hours." :question/worked-solution "**Part 1**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of $r(t) = 300t^2+50.$ To find out how many people entered the mall between $t=0$ and $t=2,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}300{t}^{2}+50\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={100{t}^{3}+50t|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(100\\cdot {2}^{3}+50(2))-(0) \\\\  & =(800+100) \\\\  & =900\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Part 2**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of $r(t) = 300t^2+50$ people per hour, and are leaving the mall at a rate of $25$ people per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of people inside the mall, which we'll call $R(t),$ is given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **net change** in the number of people, we integrate $R(t)$ between $t=0$ and $t=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}R(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)-25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)\\,\\text{d}t}_{⏟}}_{900}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =900-[25t]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =900-50 \\\\  & =850\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, over the first two hours, there was a net increase of $850$ people."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078311 :question/math-academy-id "e-434" :question/problem "![](images/36/36c6a1c84e0b0c32a5fd35fd422645f60340da6d205dd8cc31054cd558c2f5f9.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^4 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of four parts: the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0],$ a line segment for $x\\in (0,2),$ a line segment for $x\\in (2,4],$ and the point at $x=2.$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+0+{{{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle with a radius of $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (0,2).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = -A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (2,4].$  The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi -2+2 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119594 :question/math-academy-id "e-4433" :question/problem "Given that \n![](images/58/589324d1b345360020e9c2b1096b64a08008a52b05a13fdc7fd39bcb063a8d58.svg)\nfind \n![](images/f4/f4925a93ca952863cac520a853c1bc5d7c815dc8324043d6c0963d20d2b2b1ff.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{-10}+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10+1(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10-1 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064081 :question/math-academy-id "e-458" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^3\\sqrt{x^2 + 1}\\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u = x^2+1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral in terms of $u,$ and then express our final answer in terms of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\cdot x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\overset{u-1}{\\overset{⏞}{{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\overset{\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u}{\\overset{⏞}{x\\,\\text{d}x}} \\\\  & =\\int (u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (u-1){u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int ({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({x}^{2}+1)^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{5}}-\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119603 :question/math-academy-id "e-4765" :question/problem "Given ![](images/e3/e34daf05ba492435f014fedd8336dd4bb4c65ae9c1688947d7873b8fba3b3e22.svg) find its LU decomposition." :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119604 :question/math-academy-id "e-4767" :question/problem "Given \n![](images/d5/d5f838110aeb104064744379d4879fb237a2ad9ad7b4728f4216d9f1bd39cd8a.svg), find its LU decomposition." :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119605 :question/math-academy-id "e-4768" :question/problem "Given that\n![](images/ae/aea6bc2396725e1f24bbd196f15c590fbd9628dd15f220bc57653bae25840773.svg) \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119606 :question/math-academy-id "e-4769" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $F$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+R_1, \\qquad\n\tR_3:=R_3+2R_1\n$$\n\nGiven that $F$ has the LU factorization $F=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying $R_2:=R_2 + {\\color{red}1} \\cdot R_1$ and then $R_3:=R_3+ {\\color{red}2} \\cdot R_1$ is equivalent to multiplying $F$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n![](images/a9/a99ebe6a4021d9124320439383f1e5351f134a321a353c2dafaa7bbf8555aae7.svg)\n\nNow, since $E_2E_1F=U,$ we can invert $E_2$ and $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}F & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}F & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ F & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ F & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ F & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ F & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119607 :question/math-academy-id "e-4770" :question/problem "Given that\n![](images/c2/c21c2b99cdd8480ab891a23f92687d29230da7da9de9bd75a903919e54c48a1f.svg)\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{4}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_2E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_2$ and $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119610 :question/math-academy-id "e-4775" :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b},$ given by\n![](images/f1/f1124e83e3b7614557a1a6de54523cc99f62ba2a52af1da8e4d22e14466ce020.svg)\nIf $\\langle x_1,x_2 \\rangle$ and $\\langle y_1,y_2 \\rangle$ are the solutions to the systems $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ and $L\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{b}$ respectively, what is the value of $x_1 \\cdot y_2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/a1/a185a6fd91c78ed3e291b3ac8b99f9430290e5d8c1060a7d97317efc230aeb40.svg)\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n![](images/1e/1e780d384ca1fd957d742bbd047399c558ab016d187ce4cf71682546673aba35.svg)\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=6 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-8.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=6$ and $y_2=4.$ Therefore, \n![](images/58/58da62903247cf6dfdf33be86293c181ef5184048ca7cd6c921360e5d202f353.svg)\n\n- Next, we solve $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n![](images/e0/e0563dde4944ecbe2ccb98bc91246fa18c7a808df96ab77f9f8efb5510084677.svg)\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=6 \\\\ -2{x}_{2} &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x_2=-2$ and $x_1=2.$ Therefore, \n![](images/a8/a84088cff2a3589652ef7cabf09e3d8429fad7797ea20d44a416f85dcc7f9b65.svg)\n\nFinally, $x_1 \\cdot y_2 = 2 \\cdot 4 = 8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119611 :question/math-academy-id "e-4777" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $\\mathbf b,$ given by \n![](images/a1/a13a7752a224bdabc27558a6a41e2b24d3c7918af1ca4d9cff802dcb54259238.svg)\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $\\mathbf{y}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/60/605f569e0b4a54d2ca68d7b3330a2a0ebb06dcaba2b7efa911f58dbffd796fb0.svg)\n\nWe now solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n![](images/d2/d259a6740aa32e0e577756375fd85589b815232314511c74dc434a88d0146a3b.svg)\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=2 \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=4 \\\\ {y}_{1}-2{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =2, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =4-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =4-(2) \\\\  & =2, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =1-{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =1-(2)+2(2) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](images/c5/c5198aa83d3976d992b200f63941261f952282fedb241db780c20c12c97e3ba9.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119631 :question/math-academy-id "e-4823" :question/problem "Find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ if $\\,\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\},$ and\n![](images/79/798c2f477907d0d3414eeed0a2f37871c69466d9b4a098699a364f6a034c0679.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We know that any vector in  $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,\\mathcal{B}.$ Since we are told that ![](images/de/de91d9b4cd4eabed0a27696d7bf0512c19f6c66eb3c81ab6dc6a73fc0a1c15a5.svg) we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={{(-1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{{(\\,3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064102 :question/math-academy-id "e-5124" :question/problem "Given that $\\dfrac{6y − 1}{3} = ay + b,$  what are the values of $a$ and $b?$" :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{6y − 1}{3} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac{6y}{3} - \\dfrac 1 3 &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac{6}{3} \\cdot y - \\dfrac 1 3 &= \\\t\n\t\t2 \\cdot y - \\dfrac 1 3 &= \\\t\t\n\t\t2y - \\dfrac 1 3 &.\n\t}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $ay+b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{a}y+{{(-\\frac{1}{3})}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$ and $b=-\\dfrac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064103 :question/math-academy-id "e-5125" :question/problem "Given that $\\dfrac{1 − 2a + 16b}{4} = p + qa + rb,$ what are the values of $p,$ $q,$ and $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{1 − 2a + 16b}{4} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {2a} {4} + \\dfrac {16b}{4} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {2} {4} \\cdot a + \\dfrac {16}{4} \\cdot b &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {1} {2} \\cdot a + 4 \\cdot b &= \\\t\t\n\t\t\\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {a} {2} + 4b &= \\\n                \\dfrac{1}{4} - \\dfrac{1}{2}a + 4b &.\n\t}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $p+qa+rb,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(\\frac{1}{4})}_{⏟}}_{p}+{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}_{⏟}}_{q}a+{{(4)}_{⏟}}_{r}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\dfrac{1}{4},$ $q=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and $r=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175778 :question/math-academy-id "e-520" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^2\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x-1}}\\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the denominator of the integrand vanishes at $x=1$. Therefore, to evaluate the integral, we need to split it up over the point of discontinuity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{-2}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x - 1}}dx &= \\int_{-2}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x - 1}}dx_(⏟)_(I_{1}) + \\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x - 1}}dx_(⏟)_(I_{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the first integral. Using the substitution $u = x-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{1} &= \\int_{-2}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x - 1}}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{-3}^{0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{u}}du \\\\\n&= lim_(a → 0^{-})\\int_{-3}^{a}u^{-1/3}du \\\\\n&= lim_(a → 0^{-})[\\frac{3}{2}u^{2/3}]_{-3}^{a} \\\\\n&= lim_(a → 0^{-})\\frac{3}{2}[a^{2/3} - (-3)^{2/3}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}[(0)^{2/3} - \\sqrt[3]{(-3)^{2}}] \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for $I_2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{2} &= \\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x - 1}}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{u}}du \\\\\n&= lim_(a → 0^{+})\\int_{a}^{1}u^{-1/3}du \\\\\n&= lim_(a → 0^{+})[\\frac{3}{2}u^{2/3}]_{a}^{1} \\\\\n&= lim_(a → 0^{+})\\frac{3}{2}[1^{2/3} - a^{2/3}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}[1 - 0] \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{-2}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x - 1}}dx &= I_{1} + I_{2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{9} + \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}(1 - \\sqrt[3]{9})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092038 :question/math-academy-id "e-5275" :question/problem "Find the value of the constant $B$ in the trigonometric identity $\\cos^3\\theta = A\\cos3\\theta + B\\cos\\theta$." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nz = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe start with the following identities:\n\n$$\nz + \\frac{1}{z} = 2\\cos θ, z^{n} + \\frac{1}{z^{n}} = 2\\cos nθ\n$$\n\nRaising both sides to the power $3$ in the first identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(z + \\dfrac{1}{z}\\right)^3 = (2\\cos\\theta)^3 =8\\cos^3\\theta\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the binomial using the binomial theorem and simplify the result using our identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(z + \\frac{1}{z})^{3} &= z^{3} + 3z^{2}(\\frac{1}{z}) + 3z(\\frac{1}{z})^{2} + (\\frac{1}{z})^{3} \\\\\n&= z^{3} + 3z + 3(\\frac{1}{z}) + \\frac{1}{z^{3}} \\\\\n&= (z^{3} + \\frac{1}{z^{3}})_(⏟)_(2\\cos 3θ) + 3(z + \\frac{1}{z})_(⏟)_(2\\cos θ) \\\\\n&= 2\\cos 3θ + 3(2\\cos θ) \\\\\n&= 2\\cos 3θ + 6\\cos θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating $8\\cos^3\\theta$ with the above, we get\n\n$$\n8\\cos^3\\theta = 2\\cos3\\theta + 6\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $8$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\cos^3\\theta = \\frac{1}{4}\\cos3\\theta + \\frac{3}{4}\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nB=\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092039 :question/math-academy-id "e-5276" :question/problem "Given the trigonometric identity\n$\\sin^4\\theta=A\\cos4\\theta + B\\cos2\\theta+C$\nwhere $A, B$ and $C$ are constants, find the value of $A$." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nz = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe start with the following identities:\n\n$$\n(z - \\frac{1}{z}) = 2\\textrm{i}\\sin θ(z^{n} + \\frac{1}{z^{n}}) = 2\\cos nθ\n$$\n\nRaising both sides to the power $4$ in the first identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(z - \\frac{1}{z}\\right)^4 \n= (2\\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta)^4\n\n=16\\sin^4\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the binomial using the binomial theorem and simplify the result using our identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(z - \\frac{1}{z})^{4} &= z^{4} - 4z^{3}(\\frac{1}{z}) + 6z^{2}(\\frac{1}{z})^{2} - 4z(\\frac{1}{z})^{3} + (\\frac{1}{z})^{4} \\\\\n&= z^{4} - 4z^{2} + 6 - \\frac{4}{z^{2}} + \\frac{1}{z^{4}} \\\\\n&= (z^{4} + \\frac{1}{z^{4}})_(⏟)_(2\\cos 4θ) - 4(z^{2} + \\frac{1}{z^{2}})_(⏟)_(2\\cos 2θ) + 6 \\\\\n&= 2\\cos 4θ - 4(2\\cos 2θ) + 6 \\\\\n&= 2\\cos 4θ - 8\\cos 2θ + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy equating $16 \\sin^4 \\theta$ with the above, we get\n\n$$\n16\\sin^4\\theta = 2\\cos4\\theta - 8\\cos2\\theta+6\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $16$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\sin^4\\theta = \\frac{1}{8}\\cos4\\theta - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos2\\theta+\\frac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA=\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128943 :question/math-academy-id "e-5525" :question/problem "If ![](images/9e/9e1718bbf8790a9cc89d7cc1c6dd7879f8e222f348e5a9a56fec83a1b033250d.png) and ![](images/e9/e99949f7e98aee85a040f698b15822927d7aabea1e688337d209c8e1d08850d6.png) are the real and imaginary parts of the matrix ![](images/64/642f01152b6a1717300120241f788a24098413648cd70e8d0399629f11be2254.png) then what is the value of $ad+eh?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-3\\text{i} & -5-7\\text{i} \\\\ 1-6\\text{i} & 5-9\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(A)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -7 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $a=2,d=5,e=-3$ and $h=-9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nad+eh=2 \\cdot 5 + (-3) \\cdot (-9) = 37.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128945 :question/math-academy-id "e-5530" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f3cd6aeb514ee0a59d1f3534342f85e1c00743d9e7c7004b8aaf8ccfbe353f9.png)\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1-4\\textrm{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PCP^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](images/9f/9fb15e52a1df912c9e5fb4238f45d705cd0a56fb7190620c0f0fd597f9a2b8c0.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n![](images/80/80bd36915851e6ebeb67befe8af41e5496fa5491b9d87c294d8b937eb586319f.png)\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133612 :question/math-academy-id "e-5592" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where\n![](images/e0/e0a49a8ee02f2bcc10900dcaaa30969d4ab610dd703135ad4418c78272ebbca0.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, it satisfies $Q^T Q = I,$ and we get\n\n$$\nR^T R \\mathbf{x} = R^T \\! Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R^T$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $(R^T)^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=28 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=14.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=7.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}-3(7) & =28 \\\\ 7{x}_{1} & =49 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is ![](images/c3/c3f08e2cdc175b34030857c03d0301e5b734975355d09b2016559b4f28b6f5e4.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133613 :question/math-academy-id "e-5610" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n![](images/ba/ba4b73a8acd1ede70da8d25a67159c74420ac2fd11d15c830a460071d881ff42.png)\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X )^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{104}\\begin{bmatrix}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6.75 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y = 6.75 - x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133614 :question/math-academy-id "e-5611" :question/problem "An experiment consists of measuring how much a spring stretches (in inches) when one end of the spring is attached to the ceiling while a mass of $x$ ounces hangs from the other end. This data is shown below.\n\n| Mass $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Stretch $(y)$ | $0.8$ | $1.8$ | $3.2$ | $4.2$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, how much will the spring stretch if we use an $8$-ounce mass, assuming that the trend continues?  \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* \n![](images/40/400621e90f0419646dcd9e2b2e1bc31bc3cc280bef2be6c3d330c8b69308429c.png)\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 \\\\ 1.8 \\\\ 3.2 \\\\ 4.2\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X )^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}23 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 38.2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.04 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y = 0.04+ 0.82x.$\n\nFinally, to predict how much the spring will stretch if an $8$-ounce mass is hung from it,  we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.04+0.82(8) \\\\  & =6.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, according to the model, if an $8$ ounce mass is hung on the spring, the spring will stretch $6.6$ inches. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133615 :question/math-academy-id "e-5612" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as\n\n![](images/fe/fe5d2bbb69e110d08d3231d486af483dc05d995e30907a5f9a62cf46743e81d7.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$\\color{blue}1$** | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** | **$\\color{blue}5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}6$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}4$ | $\\color{red}6$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133617 :question/math-academy-id "e-5614" :question/problem "| $x_1$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$7$** | **$6.5$** | **$6$** | **$5$** | **$3$** |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n![](images/9d/9da2a49a4bab645fa1b8ea3f98e6aa6135b191e121d0cc78a73b517fbc510fc0.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find  $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." :question/worked-solution "| $x_1$ | **$\\color{blue}1$** | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** | **$\\color{blue}5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$\\color{purple}7$** | **$\\color{purple}6.5$** | **$\\color{purple}6$** | **$\\color{purple}5$** | **$\\color{purple}3$** |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}5$ | $\\color{red}9$ | $\\color{red}15$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nThus, we have\n![](images/b5/b5302251a49a45514a55d2987289548e2a55917884ff4c240be081ba512a970e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133618 :question/math-academy-id "e-5615" :question/problem "| $x_1$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$-8$** | **$-2$** | **$6$** | **$15$** |\n| $x_3$ | **$8$** | **$6$** | **$4$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $20$ | $40$ | $60$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y = \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 +\\beta_3 x_3$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n![](images/3f/3f86f03e3a32ede2ac18d6c39f62de5686a74d4050b8c1d7aa2a6fc3e2cdddbb.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find  $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." :question/worked-solution "| $x_1$ | **$\\color{blue}1$** | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$\\color{purple}-8$** | **$\\color{purple}-2$** | **$\\color{purple}6$** | **$\\color{purple}15$** |\n| $x_3$ | **$\\color{green}8$** | **$\\color{green}6$** | **$\\color{green}4$** | **$\\color{green}2$** |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}10$ | $\\color{red}20$ | $\\color{red}40$ | $\\color{red}60$ |\n\n \n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane  \n\n$$\ny= \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 +\\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 15 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 60\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nThus, we have \n\n![](images/c8/c83d300d9e6e1c1bd61fc49c4e849ab373d902beb3cd12ef501461a86bd1a08c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078326 :question/math-academy-id "e-570" :question/problem "The velocity of a particle, in meters per second, is given by $v(t) = 12-3t^2$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the time taken for the particle to travel $32\\,\\textrm m$." :question/worked-solution "To find the total distance traveled, we first need to find the points where the particle changes its direction, which can only occur when the velocity is zero. Solving $v(t) = 0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 - 3t^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n- 3(t^{2} - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nt &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sketch of $v(t)$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/ca/caea9bf724b2d9f6abc7434bebe401e28170d669390216e0b4998cf1c5d0481d.png)\n\nNotice that in the interval\n\n$$\n0\\leq t\\leq T\n$$\n\nfor $T > 2$, the curve $v(t)$ crosses the $t$-axis at $t=2$. Therefore, we need to split up the distance integral between the intervals\n\n$$\n0\\leq t\\leq 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n2\\leq t\\leq T\n$$\n\nIn the interval\n\n$$\n0\\leq t\\leq 2\n$$\n\nwe have $v(t) > 0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mid v(t) \\mid = v(t) =12-3t^2\n$$\n\nWe can work out the total distance traveled in the interval $0 < t < 2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\int_{0}^{2} \\mid v(t) \\mid dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}(12 - 3t^{2})dt \\\\\n&= \\left.(12t - t^{3})\\right|_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 12(2) - 2^{3} \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $16 < 32$, the time taken for the particle to travel $32\\,\\textrm{m}$ is greater than $2$ seconds.\n\nNow, in the interval\n\n$$\n2\\leq t\\leq T\n$$\n\nfor $T>2$, we have $v(t)<0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mid v(t) \\mid =-v(t) = 3t^2-12\n$$\n\nTherefore, an expression for the total distance $d$ traveled between $t=0$ and $t=T$ for $T >2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd(T) &= \\int_{0}^{T}\\begin{vmatrix}v(t) & dt \\\\ = \\int_{0}^{2} & v(t)\\end{vmatrix}dt_(⏟)_(16) + \\int_{2}^{T} \\mid v(t) \\mid dt \\\\\n&= 16 + \\int_{2}^{T}(3t^{2} - 12)dt \\\\\n&= 16 + \\left.(t^{3} - 12t)\\right|_{2}^{T} \\\\\n&= 16 + [(T^{3} - 12T) - ((2)^{3} - 12(2)] \\\\\n&= 16 + T^{3} - 12T - (-16) \\\\\n&= T^{3} - 12T + 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle travels $32\\,\\textrm{m}$ when $d = 32$. So, we can solve for $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{3} - 12T + 32 &= 32 \\\\\nT^{3} - 12T &= 0 \\\\\nT(T^{2} - 12) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $T=0$ and\n\n$$\nT=\\pm 2\\sqrt 3\n$$\n\nSince we require $T> 2$, the only valid solution is\n\n$$\nT=2\\sqrt 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $2\\sqrt 3$ seconds for the particle to travel a total of $32\\,\\textrm{m}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124359 :question/math-academy-id "e-5878" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-x}+c.$ Now consider the following properties of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- The domain of $f(x)$ is $x \\in (-\\infty, 2].$\n\n- The range  is $f(x) \\in [2, \\infty).$\n\n- The point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-x}+c\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b-x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\le b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in \\left(-\\infty, 2\\right],$ which is equivalent to $x\\le 2.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\nb=2.\n$$\n\n- Since the range is unbounded on the right-hand side, we know that $a>0.$ Furthermore, since we are given that the lowest point in the range is $2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{a\\sqrt{2-x}}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 0}+c\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $c=2.$\n\n- Finally, since the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =a\\sqrt{2-1}+2 \\\\ 4 & =a+2 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=2\\sqrt{2-x}+2,$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c = 2 + 2 + 2 = 6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133635 :question/math-academy-id "e-6094" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $40$ | $70$ | $120$ | $100$ | $80$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as\n\n![](images/93/93a2e368924ac5d892ce203e2b2c2ee0007ebb30bb71476272385d5b26695113.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** | **$\\color{blue}5$** | **$\\color{blue}6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}40$ | $\\color{red}70$ | $\\color{red}120$ | $\\color{red}100$ | $\\color{red}80$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2} & {6}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}40 \\\\ 70 \\\\ 120 \\\\ 100 \\\\ 80\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151955 :question/math-academy-id "e-6499" :question/problem "Let $a=85$ and $n=8.$ Find positive integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a = qn+r,$ where $0 \\leq r <          n,$ and determine $q \\cdot r.$" :question/worked-solution "Dividing $85$ by $8$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{10}} <     85\\div 8 <     11.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q={\\color{red}{10}}.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by computing $r = a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr = 85 - (10)(8) = {\\color{blue}{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $85 = qn + r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}85={{10}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 8+{{5}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $q \\cdot r = 10 \\cdot 5 = 50.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151956 :question/math-academy-id "e-6500" :question/problem "Let $a=-44$ and $n=6.$ Find integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a = qn+r,$ where $0 \\leq r <         n,$ and determine $q\\cdot r.$" :question/worked-solution "Dividing $-44$ by $6$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{-8}} <      -44\\div 6 <      -7.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q={\\color{red}{-8}}.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by solving $r = a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr = -44 - (-8)(6) = {\\color{blue}{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $-44 = qn + r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-44={{(-8)}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 6+{{4}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $q\\cdot r = -8 \\cdot 4 = -32.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151959 :question/math-academy-id "e-6560" :question/problem "If ![](images/e4/e482e62cbd229d5d7d86010ab39fea9c56ba4b1dc7bd0553cbfbb6a2fbfc8398.png) and ![](images/f4/f4542dad2dfcb51cd14caff63d99064d8a8e98772a58d4ab4022d2b572a1aad9.png) are the real and imaginary parts of the vector ![](images/8e/8e644a1eabc085ec8e7087e07d0e5860ebf1808a63747d4f9196e08b08dd72ce.png) what is the value of $ab+cd?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given vector ![](images/17/1751b58c55d6a2d44458a084d8beb31862afb4746645f10cfa04198a583f509e.png) we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3+7\\text{i} \\\\ 5-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(x)}+\\text{i}\\,{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $a=-3, b=5, c=7$ and $d=-2,$ which implies that\n\n$$\nab+cd=(-3) \\cdot 5 + 7 \\cdot (-2) = -29.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087599 :question/math-academy-id "e-6599" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\ny\\sin x\n-\n\\cos x\n=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin x$ and $Q(x) = \\cos x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228953 :question/math-academy-id "e-6617" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+y=\\tan{x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x + g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan x \\sin x\\,\\textrm{d}x\n= -\\sin x + \\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x)=-\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x)=\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=\\tan x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=\\cos x,\n\\qquad\ny_2(x)=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|)+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\,{{-\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\cos x\\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228954 :question/math-academy-id "e-6618" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny'' - 2y' + y = 18x e^{x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x} + g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ may be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x) = -\\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x) = \\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})-({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}+x{e}^{2x}-x{e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nNow compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot x{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-18\\int {x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =18\\int x\\,dx \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-6{x}^{3}){e}^{x}+(9{x}^{2})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3}{e}^{x}+9{x}^{3}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}+{{3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x) = 3x^3 e^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151972 :question/math-academy-id "e-6636" :question/problem "Find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ of the equation \n\n$$\nx^2y''+xy'   + y =  \\ln x, \\quad x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x=e^t,$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x=e^t,$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm d t} = e^t = x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac1x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}+y=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous ODE equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing.\n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is \n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A\\cos t+B\\sin t.\n$$\n\nSince right-hand side of the inhomogeneous ODE is a polynomial of degree $1,$ we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=\\alpha t+ \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\alpha\n,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}t^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n0+(\\alpha t+\\beta)=t\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha &=1\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of [math]t[/math]}) \\\\ \\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system has the solution $\\alpha=1$ and $\\beta=0.$ \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = t.\n$$\n\nNow, all that remains is to write the above solution in terms of the original variable $x.$ Since $x=e^t,$ we have $t = \\ln x,$ and therefore\n\n$$\ny_p(x) =\\ln x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157054 :question/math-academy-id "e-6877" :question/problem "Prove that if $n$ is odd, then $7n-1$ is even." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n![](images/74/74c3f2e4e2896418d438fee5f612965fe64e2bd7ecd5d7f92a24a3b7594e7092.svg)\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nIf $n$ is odd, then $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into the expression $7n-1$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n-1 & =7(2a+1)-1 \\\\  & =14a+7-1 \\\\  & =14a+6 \\\\  & =2{{(7a+3)}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=7a+3$ (highlighted above), we have that $7n-1 = 2b,$ which shows that $7n-1$ is indeed a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b = 7a+3.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n-1 & =2{{(7a+3)}_{⏟}}_{b} \\\\  & =2b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $7n-1$ is even."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198232 :question/math-academy-id "e-6878" :question/problem "Prove that if $m\\in\\mathbb Z$ is even and $n\\in\\mathbb Z$ is odd, then $mn$ is even." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nm \\text{is even and} n \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn \\text{is even}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $m$ is even and $n$ is odd.\n\n> Let $m$ and $n$ be integers, where $m$ is even and $n$ is odd. Then, $m=2a$ and $n=2b+1$ for some integers $a$ and $b$.\n\n**Watch Out**! We must use two *different* integers to represent the evenness of $m$ and the oddness of $n$. In this case, we use $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $m=2a$ and\n\n$$\nn=2b+1\n$$\n\ninto our expression $mn$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Therefore, we have\n> $mn|= (2a) \\cdot (2b + 1); = 4ab + 2a; = 2(2ab + a)_(⏟)_(c)$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nc=2ab+a\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that $mn = 2c$, which shows that $mn$ is indeed a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $c=2ab+a$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore,\n> $mn|= 2(2ab + a)_(⏟)_(c); = 2c$\n> which is a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, we conclude that $mn$ is even."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157055 :question/math-academy-id "e-6882" :question/problem "Prove that $n^2 -  2$ is odd if $n$ is odd." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n![](images/b3/b346c9920bcd64b50c80e44521c87dc10a503b09c76867f45a78854aee22ce4e.svg)\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nSince $n$ is odd, it can be expressed as $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into our expression $n^2 -  2$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nNow, writing $n^2 -  2$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-2 & =(2a+1)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+4a+1)-2 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+4a-2)+1 \\\\  & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2a-1)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=2a^2 + 2a  - 1$ (highlighted above), we have that $n^2 -  2 = 2b+1,$ which shows that $n^2 -  2$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=2a^2 + 2a  - 1.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-2 & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2a-1)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $n^2 -  2$ is odd."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157058 :question/math-academy-id "e-6900" :question/problem "Let $n$ be an integer. Using the contrapositive, prove that $n$ is odd whenever $3n-1$ is even." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\n3n-1 \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad n \\text{is odd}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\nSo, let's write down the contrapositive of our statement.\n\n> The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n> $n \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 3n-1 \\text{is odd}$\n> We'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\n> If $n$ is even, then $n=2a$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into the expression $3n-1$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2$, plus $1$.\n\n> Therefore,\n> $3n - 1|= 3(2a) - 1; = 6a - 1; = (6a - 2) + 1; = 2(3a - 1)_(⏟)_(b) + 1$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=3a-1$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n3n-1 = 2b+1\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $3n-1$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $b=3a-1$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $3n - 1|= 2(3a - 1)_(⏟)_(b) + 1; = 2b + 1$,\n> which is one larger than a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, $3n-1$ is odd."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157059 :question/math-academy-id "e-6901" :question/problem "Let $n$ be an integer. Prove that $n^3 + 1$ is even only if $n$ is odd." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $a$ such that $n = 2a$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $a$ such that $n = 2a+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n$n^3 + 1 \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad n \\text{is odd}$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\nSo, let's write down the contrapositive of our statement.\n\n> The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n> $n \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad n^3 + 1 \\text{is odd}$\n> We'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\n> If $n$ is even, then $n = 2a$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n = 2a$ into the expression $n^3 + 1$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2$, plus $1$.\n\n> Therefore,\n> $n^{3} + 1|= (2a)^{3} + 1; = 8a^{3} + 1; = 2(4a^{3})_(⏟)_(b) + 1$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b = 4a^3$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\nn^3+1 = 2b+1\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $n^3+1$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $b = 4a^3$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $n^{3} + 1|= 2(4a^{3})_(⏟)_(b) + 1; = 2b + 1$,\n> which is one larger than a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, $n^3+1$ is odd."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198233 :question/math-academy-id "e-6902" :question/problem "Let $m$ and $n$ be integers. Prove that if $mn$ is odd, then $m$ is odd or $n$ is odd." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\nmn \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m \\text{is odd or} n \\text{is odd}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\text{is odd or} n \\text{is odd}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\neg\\left(mn \\text{is odd}\\right)\n$$\n\nNegating the right-hand-side of our implication is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\text{is odd or} n \\text{is odd}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn\\text{is even}\n$$\n\nTo negate the statement \"$m$ is odd or $n$ is odd\", we use De Morgan's law for disjunctions.\n\n$$\n¬(mis odd ornis odd) \\mid ⇔¬(mis odd∨nis odd); ⇔¬(mis odd)∧¬(nis odd); ⇔mis even∧nis even; ⇔mis even andnis even\n$$\n\nSo, let's write down the contrapositive of our original statement.\n\n> The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n> $m \\text{is even and} n \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn \\text{is even}$\n> We'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the fact that $m$ and $n$ are even.\n\n> If $m$ and $n$ are even, then\n> $m = 2a \\quad\\text{and}\\quad n = 2b$,\n> where $a$ and $b$ are integers.\n\nWe substitute $m=2a$ and $n=2b$ into the expression $mn$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Therefore,\n> $mn|= (2a)(2b); = 4ab; = 2(2ab)_(⏟)_(c)$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer $c=2ab$ (highlighted above), we have that $mn= 2c$, which shows that $mn$ is indeed a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $c=2ab$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $mn|= 2(2ab)_(⏟)_(c); = 2c$,\n> which is a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, $mn$ is even."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133663 :question/math-academy-id "e-7429" :question/problem "Consider the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $\\Vert A\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert.$\n![](images/3e/3e5e0da0f8e23d412833c26dc9967e443fdccb98d347eaa0481f94294093341b.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $\\Vert A\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$ is attained when $\\mathbf{x}={\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nIn this case, $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $\\Vert A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$ is the distance between the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ and the column space.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\Vert A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}-\\mathbf{b}\\Vert =\\sqrt{(-12)^2+0^2+(-12)^2+0^2}=12\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100784 :question/math-academy-id "e-7574" :question/problem "A hotel has $21$ rooms. The manager has observed that when the room rate is $\\[math]12$ per night,  all rooms are occupied and, for every $\\[/math]1$ increase in the rate, a single room is vacated. If the maintenance of each rented room is $\\[math]3$ per night, what rate per room should the manager set to obtain the maximum profit, and what is the maximum profit?" :question/worked-solution "First, note that if there are $x$ *vacant* rooms, the hotel has $(21-x)$ occupied rooms, and the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(21-x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{occupied rooms}}\\times \\,\\,\\,{{(12+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{rate per room}} \\\\  & =252+21x-12x-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =252+9x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for having $(21-x)$ rooms rented out, the hotel has a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =3(21-x) \\\\  & =63-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =(252+9x-{x}^{2})-(63-3x) \\\\  & =189+12x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are $21$ rooms, the permitted domain is $0 \\leq x\\leq 21.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 \\lt x\\lt 21.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x) = 12 - 2x \\,.\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 12-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =12 \\\\ x & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=6$ lies in the interval $0 <  x < 21.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=6$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 <             0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=6$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=6$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the hotel will have a maximum profit when there are $x=6$ vacant rooms.  \n\nWe know that the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. If there are $6$ vacant rooms, then the rate per room is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12+6 & =\\text{\\[}math]18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the manager should charge a room rate of $\\[/math]18$ per night."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096577 :question/math-academy-id "e-7712" :question/problem "Write $7\\,800 \\times 10^{3}$ in scientific notation." :question/worked-solution "To write a number in scientific notation, we need to express it in the form\n\n$$\na\\times 10^n\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n1 \\leq a < 10\n$$\n\nand $n$ is an integer.\n\nLet's start by writing $7\\,800$ in scientific notation.\n\nTo find $a$, we place the decimal point directly after the first non-zero digit. In this case, we have the following:\n$a = 7.8$\n\nTo find $n$, we take the number $a$ that we just found and count the number of spaces we need to move the decimal point to retrieve the original number.\n\n$$\n7.8 \\rightarrow 7\\,800\n$$\n\nWe can see that the decimal point must move $3$ spaces to the *right*, so $n=3$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7\\,800 = 7.8 \\times 10^{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can express our original number in scientific notation using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7800 \\cdot 10^{3} &= 7.8 \\cdot 10^{3}_(⏟)_(7800) \\cdot 10^{3} \\\\\n&= 7.8 \\cdot 10^{3} \\cdot 10^{3} \\\\\n&= 7.8 \\cdot 10^{3 + 3} \\\\\n&= 7.8 \\cdot 10^{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166595 :question/math-academy-id "e-8340" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIf $w(x) = e^{2x}\\artanh{x},$ then $w'(x)=2e^{2x}\\artanh{x} + y(x).$ What is $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nHere, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = e^{2x}$ and $v(x)=\\artanh{x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(x) & ={({e}^{2x})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{artanh}⁡x+{e}^{2x}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\cdot \\operatorname{artanh}⁡x+{e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x+{{\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{1-{x}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{y(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{2x}}{1 - x^2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166606 :question/math-academy-id "e-8372" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIf $f(x) = \\sin{x} \\cdot \\arcsch{x},$ then $f'(x) = \\cos{x} \\arcsch{x} + g(x).$ What is $g(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIn this case, the expression for $f(x)$  is a product $u(x) \\cdot v(x)$ with $u(x) = \\sin{x}$ and $v(x) = \\arcsch{x}.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the product rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x+{{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}})}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x) = -\\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{|x|\\sqrt{1+x^2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129033 :question/math-academy-id "e-8734" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4a051da70308d92a5530fca3ba9c214cff919a6072e0e4d863f5aad10f405ee.png)\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2+4\\textrm{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PCP^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](images/4a/4a53548a17d9b4fd870ce70285bfc2c57cf6c8d352f75149e5aa7511ef4af853.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n![](images/aa/aa5738b600440bf8327670fc21cd8005c660def232119b2b4808237d988367bd.png)\n\nwhile\n\n![](images/fc/fc91fcabd47720075061e670f62be9ea9b636070a05cba70752bb7060608b764.png)\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overline{\\lambda}=2-4\\textrm{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda.$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overline{\\mathbf{v}},$ the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i} \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119706 :question/math-academy-id "e-8926" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $\\mathbf b,$ given by \n\n![](images/9d/9d0b186213211813b290f502888f68e0603bc9f77bcd7ea9d6262cb7449c00e7.svg)\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $\\mathbf{y}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/42/4298fe244fd6c66c7623ad508c97b96464321387506aa1e4be5e2399a5c9a862.svg)\n\nWe now solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 66\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=5 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=66.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=5$ and $y_2=-9.$ \n\nTherefore, ![](images/a8/a86dfd0e07b8c415e526764cf9f7ba8f020609bf7b13cc90536deba868ec4aa6.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092141 :question/math-academy-id "e-9214" :question/problem "Find the value of the constant $A$ in the trigonometric identity $\\sin^5\\theta = A\\sin5\\theta + B\\sin3\\theta + C\\sin\\theta$." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nz = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe start with the following identities:\n\n$$\n(z - \\frac{1}{z}) = 2\\textrm{i}\\sin θ(z^{n} - \\frac{1}{z^{n}}) = 2\\textrm{i}\\sin nθ\n$$\n\nRaising both sides to the power $5$ in the first identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(z - \\dfrac{1}{z}\\right)^5 \n= (2\\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta)^5\n\n=32\\textrm{i}\\sin^5\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the binomial using the binomial theorem and simplify the result using our identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(z - \\frac{1}{z})^{5} &= z^{5} - 5z^{4}(\\frac{1}{z}) + 10z^{3}(\\frac{1}{z})^{2} - 10z^{2}(\\frac{1}{z})^{3} + 5z(\\frac{1}{z})^{4} - (\\frac{1}{z})^{5} \\\\\n&= z^{5} - 5z^{3} + 10z - \\frac{10}{z} + \\frac{5}{z^{3}} - \\frac{1}{z^{5}} \\\\\n&= (z^{5} - \\frac{1}{z^{5}})_(⏟)_(2\\textrm{i}\\sin 5θ) - 5(z^{3} - \\frac{1}{z^{3}})_(⏟)_(2\\textrm{i}\\sin 3θ) + 10(z - \\frac{1}{z})_(⏟)_(2\\textrm{i}\\sin θ) \\\\\n&= 2\\textrm{i}\\sin 5θ - 5(2\\textrm{i}\\sin 3θ) + 10(2\\textrm{i}\\sin θ) \\\\\n&= 2\\textrm{i}\\sin 5θ - 10\\textrm{i}\\sin 3θ + 20\\textrm{i}\\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy equating $32\\textrm{i} \\sin^5 \\theta$ with the above, we get\n\n$$\n32\\textrm{i}\\sin^5\\theta = 2\\textrm{i}\\sin5\\theta - 10\\textrm{i}\\sin3\\theta + 20\\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $32\\textrm{i}$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\sin^5\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{16}\\sin5\\theta - \\dfrac{5}{16}\\sin3\\theta+\\dfrac{5}{8}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA=\\dfrac{1}{16}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175860 :question/math-academy-id "e-9222" :question/problem "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x, y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(5,5) = 0.9, \\quad\nF(5,2) = 0.3, \\quad\nF(0,5) = 0.45, \\quad\nF(0,2) = 0.15.\n$$\n\nFind $P(0 \\leq X \\leq 5, 2 \\leq Y \\leq 5).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $F(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/18/181df1b4d320fac30e8fc774975c8e1ae3fa6f4d360a49a38d23f1de96ebf7cc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,5).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $\\color{red}(5,2)$ and ${\\color{red}{(0,5)}}.$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at ${\\color{blue}{(0,2)}}.$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 5,2\\le Y\\le 5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](5,5)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](5,2)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 2)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](0,5)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 0,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](0,2)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 0,Y\\le 2)}} \\\\  & =F(5,5)-F(5,2)-F(0,5)+F(0,2) \\\\  & =0.9-0.3-0.45+0.15 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171898 :question/math-academy-id "e-9505" :question/problem "Which of the following are affine transformations?\n\n- ![](images/3a/3a24d08f3fce05b0a69b361d96cde0766860e0fd466a2622d780572334b2deba.png)\n\n- ![](images/d9/d910373a10dd1d08aed3df8b6b40102f4f1cc42e94e099ba6c74e6c5065828a1.png)\n\n- ![](images/05/05831230ea1993c0df5f2dae6d6411da094f181006980ea261a5b55fc9f21b40.png)" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation $\\mathbf{T}_1$ is affine. Notice that $\\mathbf{T}_1$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+v \\\\ v-2u+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+v \\\\ -2u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Transformation $\\mathbf{T}_2$ is affine. Notice that $\\mathbf{T}_2$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v \\\\ -u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn fact, the transformation $\\mathbf{T}_2$ is a linear transformation.\n\n- Transformation $\\mathbf{T}_3$ is not affine since it contains the quadratic term $v^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{T}_1$ and $\\mathbf{T}_2$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171899 :question/math-academy-id "e-9506" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx = 5u + v - 6, \\qquad y = 2u + 4v\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n![](images/f0/f017ccc60a7c1bb9ac09978492c9db217836099d0926ee009ffc44ba827c25d5.png)" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n![](images/51/514520062cf4564f58267774ba6b50027f9cd27dedfa392cb7038f94580b1e4a.png)\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5u+v-6 \\\\ 2u+4v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5u+v \\\\ 2u+4v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+2+0 = 3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171900 :question/math-academy-id "e-9508" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx = 3u-v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{a}, \\qquad y = u + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{b}v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{c}\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n![](images/43/43bcde1806155410ba97b0b2e5807d4a0f9cee93ef04f2ccfb413552de74178b.png)" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u-v \\\\ u-5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u-v+1 \\\\ u-5v\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = 3u - v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{1}, \\qquad y = u + (\\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{-5})v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{0} \\, .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+(-5)+0 = -4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179592 :question/math-academy-id "e-9520" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(k+1)!$ for any $k \\geq 2?$\n\nI. $(k+1) \\cdot k \\cdot (k-1)$\n\nII. $(k+1) \\cdot k \\cdot (k-1)!$\n\nIII. $(k+1) \\cdot k$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of factorial, we have \n\n$$\n(k+1)! = (k+1) \\cdot k \\cdot (k-1) \\cdot (k-2) \\cdot \\: \\cdots \\: \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first two factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)! & =(k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot \\overset{(k-1)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot (k-1)!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161899 :question/math-academy-id "e-9761" :question/problem "$(11.1101)_2 =$" :question/worked-solution "We can write down the binary fraction using a place value chart:\n\n| **Place Values** | $2^1$ | $2^0$ | . | $2^{-1}$ | $2^{-2}$ | $2^{-3}$ | $2^{-4}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Digits** | $1$ | $1$ | . | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, our number in base $10$ can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(10.0101)_{2} &= 1 \\cdot 2^{1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{0}^(⏞)^(whole) + 1 \\cdot 2^{-1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-2} + 0 \\cdot 2^{-3} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-4}^(⏞)^(fraction) \\\\\n&= 2 + 1 + 0.5 + 0.25 + 0 + 0.0625 \\\\\n&= (3.8125)_{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161900 :question/math-academy-id "e-9762" :question/problem "$(3.25)_{10} =$" :question/worked-solution "We express the number $(3.25)_{10}$ in base $10$ as follows:\n\n- For the whole number part ($3$), we extract the powers of $2$ from the number, starting from the largest possible one.\n- For the fractional part, we extract the *negative* powers of $2$ from the number, starting from the largest possible one.\n\nTherefore, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3.25 &= 3^(⏞)^(whole) + 0.25^(⏞)^(fraction) \\\\\n&= 2^{1} + 2^{0} + 2^{-2} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 2^{1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{0}_(⏟)_(whole) + 0 \\cdot 2^{-1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-2}_(⏟)_(fraction)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding place value chart is as follows:\n\n| **Place Values** | $2^{1}$ | $2^{0}$ | . | $2^{-1}$ | $2^{-2}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Digits** | $1$ | $1$ | . | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(3.25)_{10} = (11.01)_2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161901 :question/math-academy-id "e-9763" :question/problem "Find the first six digits of $(0.9)_{10}$ in base $2$." :question/worked-solution "We extract the negative powers of $2$ from the number, starting from the largest possible one:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.9 &= 0.5_(⏟)_(1/2) + 0.4 \\\\\n&= 0.5_(⏟)_(1/2) + 0.25_(⏟)_(1/4) + 0.15 \\\\\n&= 0.5_(⏟)_(1/2) + 0.25_(⏟)_(1/4) + 0.125_(⏟)_(1/8) + 0.025 \\\\\n&= 0.5_(⏟)_(1/2) + 0.25_(⏟)_(1/4) + 0.125_(⏟)_(1/8) + 0.015625_(⏟)_(1/64) + 0.009375 \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 2^{-1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-2} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-3} + 0 \\cdot 2^{-4} + 0 \\cdot 2^{-5} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-6} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding place value chart is as follows:\n\n| **Place Values** | $2^{-1}$ | $2^{-2}$ | $2^{-3}$ | $2^{-4}$ | $2^{-5}$ | $2^{-6}$ | $\\ldots$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Digits** | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\ldots$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(0.9)_{10} = (0.\\,111\\:001 \\ldots)_2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157226 :question/math-academy-id "e-9766" :question/problem "What is $(56.6)_8$ written in base $10$?" :question/worked-solution "We can write down the number using a place value chart:\n\n| **Place Values** | $8^1$ | $8^0$ | . | $8^{-1}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Digits** | $5$ | $6$ | . | $6$ |\n\nTherefore, our number can be written in base $10$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(56.6)_{8} &= 5 \\cdot 8^{1} + 6 \\cdot 8^{0}^(⏞)^(whole) + 6 \\cdot 8^{-1}^(⏞)^(fraction) \\\\\n&= 40 + 6 + 0.75 \\\\\n&= (46.75)_{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157228 :question/math-academy-id "e-9769" :question/problem "What is $(4.31)_7$ written as a fraction in base $10$?" :question/worked-solution "We can write down the number using a place value chart:\n\n| **Place Values** | $7^0$ | . | $7^{-1}$ | $7^{-2}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Digits** | $4$ | . | $3$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, our number can be written in base $10$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4.31)_{7} &= 4 \\cdot 7^{0}^(⏞)^(whole) + 3 \\cdot 7^{-1} + 1 \\cdot 7^{-2}^(⏞)^(fraction) \\\\\n&= 4 + \\frac{3}{7} + \\frac{1}{49} \\\\\n&= \\frac{218}{49}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133718 :question/math-academy-id "e-9914" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-6$** | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-7$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n![](images/80/8002d38943a0f0b72e5c9cef2e3a455915479ca86c53ebe403b60a6b49f268a4.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Which are the design matrix $X$ and the observation vector $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$\\color{blue}-6$** | **$\\color{blue}-3$** | **$\\color{blue}-1$** | **$\\color{blue}0$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}-7$ | $\\color{red}-4$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}8$ | $\\color{red}10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188216 :question/math-academy-id "e-9987" :question/problem "Find $\\text{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})$ given that $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3\\to\\mathbb{R}^3$ and $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\{ [ x, y, z ]^T \\, : \\, x=y=z\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Any vector from $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ x \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}=x{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}.$ Therefore, $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=1.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120080 :question/math-academy-id "q-105949" :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-1 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-1$ and ${y}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=y=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=2$ and ${x}_{1}=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{1}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055060 :question/math-academy-id "q-175911" :question/problem "$\\underset{A}{\\underbrace{2 \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} 8}} \\hspace{1em} \\underset{\\land }{\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p} \\mathbf{\\text{and}}}} \\hspace{1em} \\underset{B}{\\underbrace{2 \\vphantom{p} \\text{ is prime}}}$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$A$ is {{field-1}},\n$B$ is {{field-2}},\n$A∧B$ is {{field-3}}."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055061 :question/math-academy-id "q-178152" :question/problem "$$\n{{\\overset{P}{\\overset{⏞}{{3}^{2}=9}}\\,\\overset{∧}{\\overset{⏞}{\\text{and}}}\\,\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{2\\text{ is rational}}}}_{⏟}}_{P\\,∧\\,Q}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{\\sqrt{5}\\text{ is irrational}}_{⏟}}_{R}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $P∧Q$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $R$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $(P∧Q)∧R$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is true, $Q$ is true, and $R$ is true. Therefore, $P∧Q$ is true, and the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P∧Q)∧R & ≡\\overset{\\text{true}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{true}∧\\text{true})}}∧\\text{true} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}∧\\text{true} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055087 :question/math-academy-id "q-181713" :question/problem "$$\n{{2\\text{ is negative}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{0.5\\text{ is rational}}_{⏟}}_{B}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $A$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $B$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $A∨B$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $A$ is false, and $B$ is true. Therefore, the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is negative}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{0.5\\text{ is rational}}_{⏟}}_{B} & \\,\\,≡\\,\\text{false}∨\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055088 :question/math-academy-id "q-181714" :question/problem "$$\n{{2\\text{ is prime}}_{⏟}}_{R}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{\\overset{S}{\\overset{⏞}{7\\,|\\,14}}\\,\\overset{∧}{\\overset{⏞}{\\text{and}}}\\,\\overset{T}{\\overset{⏞}{5\\,|\\,14}}}_{⏟}}_{S\\,∧\\,T}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $R$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $S∧T$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $R∨(S∧T)$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $R$ is true, $S$ is true, and $T$ is false. Therefore, $S∧T$ is false, but the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R∨(S∧T) & ≡\\text{true}∨\\overset{\\text{false}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{true}∧\\text{false})}} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}∨\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120185 :question/math-academy-id "q-32539" :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$, given by\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(L)\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(U), b = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf $⟨x_{1}, x_{2}⟩$ and $⟨y_{1}, y_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax = b$ and $Ly = b$ respectively, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot y_{2}$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120205 :question/math-academy-id "q-32726" :question/problem "$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\boxed{} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry ${l}_{31}$ in the matrix $L?$" :question/worked-solution "We want to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{31}$ using Gaussian elimination. To do this, we take the $3$rd row of $A,$ and we add the $1$st row of $A$ multiplied by $-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe entry ${l}_{31}$ of the matrix $L$ is the factor next to ${R}_{1},$ taken with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{l}_{31}=-(-2)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120206 :question/math-academy-id "q-32772" :question/problem "$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. From left to right, what are the missing elements from the second row of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{21}$ of the matrix $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the elements from the second row of $U$ are $0,-5,$ and $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120208 :question/math-academy-id "q-32805" :question/problem "$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 12 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 7 & -12\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & \\boxed{} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -17\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry ${l}_{32}$ in the matrix $L?$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make zeros below the main diagonal, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 12 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 7 & -12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 20 \\\\ 9 & 7 & -12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{32}$ using Gaussian elimination. To do this, we take the $3$rd row of $A,$ and we add the $2$nd row of $A$ multiplied by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -17\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe entry ${l}_{32}$ of the matrix $L$ is the factor next to ${R}_{2},$ taken with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}=-(-1)=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120209 :question/math-academy-id "q-32806" :question/problem "$\\underset{A}{\\underbrace{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}4 & 2 & - 2 \\\\ 16 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 4 & - 2 & 8\\end{array} \\right]}} = \\underset{L}{\\underbrace{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}} \\cdot  \\underset{U}{\\underbrace{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}4 & 2 & - 2 \\\\ 0 & - 4 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\boxed{\\phantom{\\hspace{0.167em} N \\hspace{0.167em}}}\\end{array} \\right]}} .$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of $u_{33}$ in the matrix $U$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167185 :question/math-academy-id "q-330893" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-10{e}^{-2t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2{e}^{4t}-(-3{e}^{4t})\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{4t} & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-6t}}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{4t} & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}-10{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int -10{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -4{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167186 :question/math-academy-id "q-330899" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & -6{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{-t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & -6{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-6{e}^{2t})-5{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}\\begin{bmatrix}-6{e}^{2t} & -5{e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-6{e}^{2t} & -5{e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-6{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-6{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}12{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 12{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & -6{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5{e}^{t} \\\\ 9{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134316 :question/math-academy-id "q-40812" :question/problem "$\\overset{A}{\\overbrace{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & - 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}} = \\overset{Q}{\\overbrace{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {q}_{22}} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{array} \\right]}} \\overset{R}{\\overbrace{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {r}_{22}}\\end{array} \\right]}} .$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns $a_{1}, a_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns $q_{1}, q_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A$, what is the value of $q_{22} \\cdot r_{22}$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134324 :question/math-academy-id "q-40838" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{12}{6})\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{6})=2\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134326 :question/math-academy-id "q-40845" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{9}{9})\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (-3)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134352 :question/math-academy-id "q-41415" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{2}+2{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{3}-4{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{2}+2{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{3}-4{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &=2{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=-8{q}_{1}+4{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=24{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+6{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 & 24 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}=-8+4+(-6)=-10.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134354 :question/math-academy-id "q-41417" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=4,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=2,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=6,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-2{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-4{v}_{1}+3{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{4}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=2{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{6}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=6{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}\\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-2{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-4{v}_{1}+3{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &={v}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=8{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=4{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=16{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+6{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=4+16+6=26.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134356 :question/math-academy-id "q-41419" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot 0=0.$"}]}
